ings about imposter  syndrome is that it's really easy to get  in your head and be overly critical  about yourself  because no one is stopping you from  doing so and it's really hard to be  objective about yourself like everyone's  their harshest critic and so you end up  kind of like beating yourself  up and talking yourself down all the way  down  and so one thing that i think is really  helpful is to talk to  a friend or a manager or a trusted  colleague  who understands your work or your  accomplishments  to listen to your worries because first  of all  verbalizing your fears makes them real  which i know that in and of itself is  scary but at least you're facing it  if there's one thing i've learned from  experiencing crippling anxiety sometimes  is that  you do have to face your fears because  if you keep ignoring it  and you just think that it doesn't exist  or something then it doesn't actually  address the fear  at all and once you tell that to someone  then likely your friend  trusted colleague manager whoever is  gonna hype you up and support you  so hard or even just by like verbalizing  it to like a wall  you'll realize how critical you are of  yourself  like think about if you heard a dear  friend or family member be  super critical of themselves you would  stop them right and be like yo like what  the heck are you talking about like  that's totally not true  i've seen you do x y and z like you're  gonna do this you're just  you're great you're totally fine or  whatever you would say to your friend i  don't know that's what i would say but  maybe we'd use some different words  and all this kind of leads into like my  last piece of advice is to yeah  figure out what you're afraid of and  then make a plan  usually when i'm experiencing imposter  syndrome it's just that everything is  entirely overwhelming and i can't even  see the details of what i'm afraid of  and sometimes i know exactly what i'm  afraid of and what i'm feeling impostery  about  but it